/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - At a point, choose axes so that the shear stresses vanish.
   - A ductile material fails by yielding, so it is checked against Syt.
   - σeq ≤ Syt/n (ductile) σeq ≤ Sut/n (brittle)
   - σeq is the equivalent stress in MPa; Syt is the tensile yield strength; Sut is the ultimate tensile strength; Suc is the ultimate compressive strength (all in MPa); n is the factor of safety, a pure number.
   - Each theory names a different cause of failure. ν is Poisson's ratio (about 0.3 for steel); Ssy is the shear yield strength the theory predicts.
   - Hydrostatic stress (equal on all three planes) changes volume but not shape and does not make a ductile metal yield; only the distortion energy theory leaves it out completely, which is why it fits ductile tests best.
   - In plane stress the Tresca safe region is a hexagon inside the von Mises ellipse, which passes through the same six corners, so Tresca is the safer of the two.
   - Choice rule: ductile steel, von Mises (most codes) or Tresca for a quick, safe answer; brittle cast iron, Rankine for mainly tensile stresses and Coulomb–Mohr when tension and compression both act.
   - σ1,2 = (σx + σy)/2 ± √(((σx − σy)/2)² + τxy²)
   - σx, σy and τxy are the plane stresses in MPa (Mohr's circle).
*/
