/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Far from a notch the stress follows the simple formula; at the notch root it rises to a sharp peak.
   - Kt = σmax/σnom
   - σmax is the highest local elastic stress at the notch and σnom is the nominal stress on the net section at the notch for the same load, both in MPa.
   - Take σnom on the net section, because the charts were drawn that way.
   - An elliptical hole in a wide plate has Kt = 1 + 2a/b (a the half-axis across the load, b along it): a circle gives 3 and a thin slit a very large value, which is why cracks grow.
   - Under repeated load the fatigue strength of a notched bar falls by a factor Kf that is usually smaller than Kt, because the steep stress gradient at a small notch leaves the material around it to help.
   - q = (Kf − 1)/(Kt − 1), so Kf = 1 + q(Kt − 1)
   - q runs from 0 (the material ignores the notch, Kf = 1) to 1 (full sensitivity, Kf = Kt), so Kf always lies between 1 and Kt.
   - Data books give q against notch radius r for each class of steel.
   - Under a steady load a ductile part yields locally at the notch, the peak spreads out, and design on the nominal stress is usually enough.
*/
