/* book exam traps: Using Kt in a fatigue calculation. Fatigue uses Kf = 1 + q(Kt − 1), which is smaller unless q = 1. | Taking σnom on the gross area: in the plate example 60 MPa instead of 75 MPa, a peak 20 % too low. | Reading the bending chart for a torsion load. The curves differ for axial, bending and torsion. | Calling Kt a material property. Kt depends only on geometry and load type; q (and so Kf) depends on the material and the radius. | Applying the notch twice: multiplying the stress by Kf after the data book has already divided Se by Kf. */
